# Exploring TypeScript Patterns for Reactive Programming

Reactive programming has gained significant traction in the world of software development due to its ability to handle asynchronous streams of data efficiently. TypeScript, being a superset of JavaScript, provides a robust foundation for building reactive applications. This article delves into various TypeScript patterns that facilitate reactive programming, ensuring better performance, maintainability, and scalability of applications.

## Table of Contents

- [Introduction](#introduction)
- [Reactive Programming in TypeScript](#reactive-programming-in-typescript)
  - [Observables](#observables)
  - [Operators](#operators)
- [Patterns for Reactive Programming](#patterns-for-reactive-programming)
  - [Subject Pattern](#subject-pattern)
  - [Behavior Subject Pattern](#behavior-subject-pattern)
  - [Replay Subject Pattern](#replay-subject-pattern)
  - [Async Subject Pattern](#async-subject-pattern)
  - [CombineLatest Pattern](#combinelatest-pattern)
  - [Zip Pattern](#zip-pattern)
- [Comparison Table](#comparison-table)
- [Actionable Takeaways](#actionable-takeaways)

## Introduction

Reactive programming allows developers to handle asynchronous streams of data with ease. TypeScript, with its static typing and strong typing features, provides a powerful toolset for implementing reactive patterns. By leveraging TypeScript patterns, developers can create robust, scalable, and maintainable reactive applications.

## Reactive Programming in TypeScript

Before diving into patterns, it's essential to understand the core concepts of reactive programming in TypeScript.

### Observables

An observable is a sequence of values that emits values over time. In TypeScript, observables are typically created using the RxJS library. An observable can be created from a wide range of sources, such as arrays, timers, and HTTP requests.

```typescript
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';

const observable = new Observable(subscriber => {
  subscriber.next(1);
  subscriber.next(2);
  subscriber.next(3);
  subscriber.complete();
});

observable.subscribe(value => console.log(value));
```

### Operators

Operators in RxJS allow you to transform and combine observables. Some common operators include `map`, `filter`, `switchMap`, and `mergeMap`.

```typescript
import { map } from 'rxjs/operators';

const observable = new Observable(subscriber => {
  subscriber.next(1);
  subscriber.next(2);
  subscriber.next(3);
});

const transformedObservable = observable.pipe(map(value => value * 2));

transformedObservable.subscribe(value => console.log(value));
```

## Patterns for Reactive Programming

Now that we have a basic understanding of reactive programming in TypeScript, let's explore some patterns that can help us build efficient and maintainable applications.

### Subject Pattern

The subject pattern is a fundamental reactive pattern that allows us to emit values to multiple subscribers. In TypeScript, subjects are implemented using the `Subject` class from RxJS.

```typescript
import { Subject } from 'rxjs';

const subject = new Subject<number>();

subject.next(1);
subject.next(2);
subject.next(3);

subject.subscribe(value => console.log('First subscriber:', value));
subject.subscribe(value => console.log('Second subscriber:', value));
```

### Behavior Subject Pattern

The behavior subject pattern is a variant of the subject pattern that retains the latest value and emits it to new subscribers. This pattern is useful when you want to maintain the state of an application.

```typescript
import { BehaviorSubject } from 'rxjs';

const behaviorSubject = new BehaviorSubject<number>(0);

behaviorSubject.next(1);
behaviorSubject.next(2);
behaviorSubject.next(3);

behaviorSubject.subscribe(value => console.log('First subscriber:', value));
behaviorSubject.subscribe(value => console.log('Second subscriber:', value));
```

### Replay Subject Pattern

The replay subject pattern is similar to the behavior subject pattern but also retains a buffer of emitted values and emits this buffer to new subscribers. This pattern is useful when you want to provide a history of values to new subscribers.

```typescript
import { ReplaySubject } from 'rxjs';

const replaySubject = new ReplaySubject<number>(2);

replaySubject.next(1);
replaySubject.next(2);
replaySubject.next(3);

replaySubject.subscribe(value => console.log('First subscriber:', value));
replaySubject.subscribe(value => console.log('Second subscriber:', value));
```

### Async Subject Pattern

The async subject pattern is a variant of the subject pattern that emits values to subscribers only after the observable completes. This pattern is useful when you want to ensure that all subscribers receive the same value at the same time.

```typescript
import { AsyncSubject } from 'rxjs';

const asyncSubject = new AsyncSubject<number>();

asyncSubject.next(1);
asyncSubject.next(2);
asyncSubject.next(3);

asyncSubject.subscribe(value => console.log('First subscriber:', value));
asyncSubject.complete();
```

### CombineLatest Pattern

The combineLatest pattern allows you to combine multiple observables and emit values only when all observables have emitted at least one value. This pattern is useful when you want to work with multiple data sources simultaneously.

```typescript
import { combineLatest } from 'rxjs';
import { of } from 'rxjs';

const observable1 = of(1, 2, 3);
const observable2 = of('a', 'b', 'c');

combineLatest([observable1, observable2]).subscribe(([value1, value2]) => {
  console.log(value1, value2);
});
```

### Zip Pattern

The zip pattern allows you to combine multiple observables and emit values only when all observables have emitted a value. Unlike the combineLatest pattern, the zip pattern waits for all observables to emit a value before emitting the next value.

```typescript
import { zip } from 'rxjs';
import { of } from 'rxjs';

const observable1 = of(1, 2, 3);
const observable2 = of('a', 'b', 'c');

zip(observable1, observable2).subscribe(([value1, value2]) => {
  console.log(value1, value2);
});
```

## Comparison Table

| Pattern         | Description                                                                 | Use Case                                                                                   |
|-----------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Subject         | Basic reactive pattern to emit values to subscribers.                          | When you need to emit values to multiple subscribers.                                        |
| Behavior Subject| Retains the latest value and emits it to new subscribers.                       | When you want to maintain the state of an application.                                       |
| Replay Subject  | Retains a buffer of emitted values and emits this buffer to new subscribers. | When you want to provide a history of values to new subscribers.                            |
| Async Subject   | Emits values to subscribers only after the observable completes.                | When you want to ensure that all subscribers receive the same value at the same time.        |
| CombineLatest   | Combines multiple observables and emits values only when all observables emit. | When you want to work with multiple data sources simultaneously.                             |
| Zip             | Combines multiple observables and emits values only when all observables emit. | When you want to wait for all observables to emit a value before emitting the next value.    |

## Actionable Takeaways

- Familiarize yourself with the core concepts of reactive programming and TypeScript.
- Understand the different types of subjects and their use cases.
- Utilize operators to transform and combine observables effectively.
- Choose the appropriate pattern based on your application's requirements.
- Test and optimize your reactive applications for better performance and maintainability.

By leveraging TypeScript patterns for reactive programming, developers can create robust, scalable, and maintainable applications. Stay tuned for more articles on TypeScript and reactive programming.

# Webhook Security: Signing and Verification in TypeScript

Webhooks deliver events from third-party services (payment providers, CI/CD, SaaS) to your backend. Without verification, anyone can POST to your endpoint and impersonate the provider. This post shows how to sign and verify webhooks in TypeScript so only legitimate payloads are processed.

## Why Sign Webhooks?

A public webhook URL is guessable. Attackers can:

- Send fake "payment succeeded" or "subscription cancelled" events.
- Trigger side effects (refunds, emails, state changes).
- Probe your system with malformed or oversized payloads.

Signing binds each request to a shared secret. Your server recomputes the signature from the raw body and compares it to the header; if they match, the payload is authentic and unchanged.

## Standard: HMAC in a Header

Most providers (Stripe, GitHub, Slack, etc.) use HMAC-SHA256:

1. They compute `HMAC-SHA256(secret, rawRequestBody)`.
2. They send the hex (or base64) digest in a header, e.g. `X-Webhook-Signature` or `Stripe-Signature`.
3. You do the same with your copy of the secret and the exact raw body; if the result equals the header (or passes their scheme), you accept the request.

Critical: you must use the **raw request body** (string or buffer), not a parsed JSON object, because parsing and re-serializing can change bytes and break the signature.

## Example: Generic Verifier

```typescript
import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from "node:crypto";

const WEBHOOK_SECRET = process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET!;

function verifyWebhookSignature(
  rawBody: string | Buffer,
  signatureHeader: string,
  secret: string = WEBHOOK_SECRET
): boolean {
  const expected = createHmac("sha256", secret)
    .update(rawBody)
    .digest("hex");
  const provided = signatureHeader.replace(/^sha256=/, "").trim();
  if (expected.length !== provided.length) return false;
  return timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(expected, "hex"), Buffer.from(provided, "hex"));
}
```

Use `timingSafeEqual` so comparison time does not leak information about the correct signature. Always ensure the two buffers have the same length before calling it to avoid throwing.

## Getting the Raw Body

In Express you need the unparsed body. Either:

- Use a middleware that stores the raw body before `express.json()`, or
- Use `express.raw({ type: "application/json" })` for the webhook route and parse JSON yourself after verification.

Example with a dedicated webhook route and `express.raw`:

```typescript
app.post(
  "/webhooks/payments",
  express.raw({ type: "application/json" }),
  (req, res) => {
    const signature = req.headers["x-webhook-signature"] as string;
    const rawBody = req.body; // Buffer
    if (!verifyWebhookSignature(rawBody, signature)) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: "Invalid signature" });
    }
    const payload = JSON.parse(rawBody.toString("utf-8"));
    // Process payload...
    res.status(200).end();
  }
);
```

In Hono or other frameworks, read the body as text or buffer before any JSON parsing and pass that to the verifier.

## Provider-Specific Details

- **Stripe**: Header `Stripe-Signature` contains one or more `t=timestamp,v1=signature` pairs. You verify the `v1` signature using the timestamp and body to prevent replay; Stripe’s docs give the exact format.
- **GitHub**: Header `X-Hub-Signature-256` with `sha256=<hex>`. Verify with the raw body and your webhook secret.
- **Slack**: Header `X-Slack-Signature` with `v0=<hex>`. Same idea: HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body with your signing secret.

Always read the provider’s documentation for the exact header name, encoding (hex vs base64), and whether they include a timestamp for replay protection.

## Replay Protection

If the signature only covers the body, an attacker could resend the same request. Many providers include a timestamp in the signed payload or in a header. Your server should reject requests whose timestamp is too old (e.g. more than 5 minutes). Stripe does this with the `t=` value in their signature header.

## Summary

Verify every webhook by recomputing HMAC-SHA256 over the **raw body** with your shared secret and comparing it to the provider’s header using a constant-time comparison. Use the raw body buffer or string and follow each provider’s docs for header format and replay rules. This keeps your system safe from forged and replayed webhook calls.

