# Running OpenClaw + Cursor Agent as a 24/7 Unattended System

Enterprise AI agents need to operate continuously without human intervention. When Raj Patel, lead automation engineer at a mid-size logistics company in Hamburg, Germany, needed to deploy an AI-powered document processing system that ran around the clock, he faced a challenge common to many teams: how to keep AI agents running reliably without constant supervision.

This guide walks through the architecture and implementation strategies for running OpenClaw and Cursor Agent as an unattended 24/7 system. You will learn how to implement heartbeat monitoring, task scheduling, and error recovery mechanisms that keep your AI agents productive while you focus on other work.

The techniques covered here apply to any AI agent deployment, but they are particularly relevant for OpenClaw users given its rich configuration options for unattended operation. Whether you are processing documents like Raj, monitoring systems, or handling customer inquiries across time zones, the principles remain the same.

## The Real-World Challenge

Raj's team processes over 2,000 shipping documents daily. Their AI agent needed to monitor email inboxes, extract data from attachments, validate information against their ERP system, and flag exceptions for human review. The system had to work continuously because their customers spanned multiple time zones, from Tokyo to San Francisco.

Initial attempts with simple daemon processes failed within hours. Memory leaks caused gradual degradation. Network blips triggered unhandled exceptions. Weekend maintenance windows created data bottlenecks. Raj needed a robust architecture that could handle these challenges gracefully.

A similar situation faced Sofia Andersson, DevOps lead at a healthcare technology company in Stockholm, Sweden. Her team deployed an AI agent to monitor patient record access logs and detect potential security breaches. The agent had to run continuously to meet compliance requirements for rapid breach detection. When the initial implementation failed during a weekend in early November 2025, they lost 48 hours of log data—exactly the kind of data needed for security investigations.

Both Raj and Sofia needed the same fundamental capabilities: reliable heartbeat monitoring, intelligent task scheduling, and robust error recovery. The implementation details differed, but the architectural patterns were identical.

## Heartbeat Implementation

The heartbeat mechanism serves as the vital sign monitor for your AI agent. It confirms the agent remains alive, responsive, and capable of processing work. Without proper heartbeat implementation, a failed agent can run undetected for hours, accumulating backlog and missing critical deadlines.

OpenClaw supports configurable heartbeat intervals through the agent configuration. Setting the heartbeat to "0" enables continuous monitoring mode, where the agent reports status after every operation completes.

```typescript
import { writeFileSync, readFileSync, existsSync } from 'fs';
import { setInterval } from 'timers';

interface HeartbeatConfig {
  every: number;
  capabilities: {
    inlineButtons: 'all' | 'limited' | 'none';
  };
}

const agentHeartbeat: HeartbeatConfig = {
  every: 0,
  capabilities: {
    inlineButtons: 'all'
  }
};

class AgentHeartbeatExporter {
  private lastHeartbeat: Date = new Date();
  private readonly endpoint: string;
  private readonly intervalMs: number = 30000;
  private readonly agentId: string;
  private readonly healthCheckFile: string = '/tmp/agent-health.json';

  constructor(endpoint: string, agentId: string) {
    this.endpoint = endpoint;
    this.agentId = agentId;
  }

  private collectMetrics(): object {
    const memUsage = process.memoryUsage();
    return {
      agentId: this.agentId,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      uptime: process.uptime(),
      memoryUsage: {
        heapUsed: Math.round(memUsage.heapUsed / 1024 / 1024),
        heapTotal: Math.round(memUsage.heapTotal / 1024 / 1024),
        rss: Math.round(memUsage.rss / 1024 / 1024)
      },
      status: 'healthy'
    };
  }

  private writeHealthFile(metrics: object): void {
    try {
      writeFileSync(this.healthCheckFile, JSON.stringify(metrics, null, 2));
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Failed to write health file:', error);
    }
  }

  async report(): Promise<void> {
    const payload = this.collectMetrics();

    this.writeHealthFile(payload);

    try {
      const response = await fetch(this.endpoint, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify(payload),
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000)
      });

      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
      }

      this.lastHeartbeat = new Date();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Heartbeat export failed:', error);
    }
  }

  start(): void {
    setInterval(() => this.report(), this.intervalMs);
    console.log(`Heartbeat exporter started, reporting to ${this.endpoint} every ${this.intervalMs}ms`);
  }
}

const exporter = new AgentHeartbeatExporter(
  process.env.HEARTBEAT_ENDPOINT || 'https://monitoring.example.com/heartbeat',
  process.env.OPENCLAW_AGENT_ID || 'doc-processor'
);
exporter.start();
```

The key insight here is separating the agent's internal heartbeat from external monitoring. OpenClaw's built-in heartbeat manages agent-to-coordinator communication, while the exporter handles alerting and dashboards for your operations team. This separation also allows the health check file to be read by external process supervisors like systemd or container orchestrators.

## Task Scheduling Architecture

Effective task scheduling for 24/7 agents requires a three-tier approach: immediate processing for urgent work, scheduled batches for predictable loads, and queue-based handling for background tasks. OpenClaw's cron system provides the foundation, while custom queue management handles the nuanced prioritization.

```typescript
import * as cron from 'node-cron';
import { setTimeout } from 'timers/promises';

interface ScheduledTask {
  id: string;
  cronExpression: string;
  handler: () => Promise<void>;
  retryPolicy: RetryPolicy;
  timeoutMs: number;
}

interface RetryPolicy {
  maxAttempts: number;
  backoffMs: number;
  backoffMultiplier: number;
}

class TaskScheduler {
  private tasks: Map<string, ScheduledTask> = new Map();
  private runningTasks: Set<string> = new Set();

  register(task: ScheduledTask): void {
    this.tasks.set(task.id, task);
    this.scheduleCron(task);
    console.log(`Registered task: ${task.id} with schedule: ${task.cronExpression}`);
  }

  private scheduleCron(task: ScheduledTask): void {
    cron.schedule(task.cronExpression, async () => {
      if (this.runningTasks.has(task.id)) {
        console.warn(`Task ${task.id} is already running, skipping this run`);
        return;
      }

      this.runningTasks.add(task.id);
      try {
        await this.executeWithTimeout(task);
      } catch (error) {
        await this.handleFailure(task, error);
      } finally {
        this.runningTasks.delete(task.id);
      }
    });
  }

  private async executeWithTimeout(task: ScheduledTask): Promise<void> {
    await Promise.race([
      task.handler(),
      setTimeout(task.timeoutMs, undefined, { ref: false }).then(() => {
        throw new Error(`Task ${task.id} timed out after ${task.timeoutMs}ms`);
      })
    ]);
  }

  private async handleFailure(task: ScheduledTask, error: Error): Promise<void> {
    const retryPolicy = task.retryPolicy;

    for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= retryPolicy.maxAttempts; attempt++) {
      const baseDelay = retryPolicy.backoffMs * Math.pow(retryPolicy.backoffMultiplier, attempt - 1);
      const jitter = Math.random() * baseDelay * 0.3;
      const delay = baseDelay + jitter;

      console.log(`Task ${task.id} attempt ${attempt}/${retryPolicy.maxAttempts} failed, retrying in ${Math.round(delay)}ms`);

      await setTimeout(delay);

      try {
        await task.handler();
        console.log(`Task ${task.id} succeeded on attempt ${attempt}`);
        return;
      } catch (attemptError) {
        console.error(`Attempt ${attempt} failed:`, attemptError);
      }
    }

    await this.notifyFailure(task, error);
  }

  private async notifyFailure(task: ScheduledTask, error: Error): Promise<void> {
    console.error(`Task ${task.id} failed permanently after ${task.retryPolicy.maxAttempts} attempts:`, error.message);
  }
}
```

This scheduler handles three critical scenarios. First, it respects timeout boundaries to prevent runaway tasks from consuming resources indefinitely. Second, it implements exponential backoff with jitter to give transient failures a chance to resolve while avoiding the "thundering herd" problem. Third, it escalates to notification after exhausting retries, ensuring your team knows about persistent problems.

