# Comparing TypeScript Functional Programming to OOP: When and Why to Use Each

Functional programming (FP) and object-oriented programming (OOP) are two of the most popular paradigms in software development. Each has its strengths and weaknesses, and the choice between them often depends on the specific requirements of a project. In this article, we will explore TypeScript, a language that supports both paradigms, and discuss when and why to use each.

## Introduction

TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static types and additional features. It is designed to be a transpiled language, meaning that TypeScript code is compiled to JavaScript, which can then be run in any JavaScript environment. TypeScript's support for both FP and OOP makes it a versatile choice for developers.

## Functional Programming in TypeScript

Functional programming emphasizes the use of pure functions, immutability, and higher-order functions. Here are some key aspects of functional programming in TypeScript:

### Pure Functions

Pure functions are functions that always return the same output for the same input and have no side effects. They are easier to test and reason about.

```typescript
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}
```

### Immutability

Immutability means that data is not changed after it is created. This makes it easier to track changes and debug issues.

```typescript
let number = 5;
number = number + 1; // This is not functional programming
```

### Higher-Order Functions

Higher-order functions are functions that take other functions as arguments or return functions as results.

```typescript
function map<T, U>(array: T[], callback: (item: T) => U): U[] {
  return array.map(callback);
}

const numbers = [1, 2, 3];
const doubledNumbers = map(numbers, (number) => number * 2);
```

## Object-Oriented Programming in TypeScript

Object-oriented programming focuses on the use of classes, objects, and inheritance. Here are some key aspects of OOP in TypeScript:

### Classes and Objects

Classes are blueprints for creating objects. Objects are instances of classes and can have properties and methods.

```typescript
class Person {
  name: string;
  age: number;

  constructor(name: string, age: number) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
  }

  greet() {
    console.log(`Hello, my name is ${this.name} and I am ${this.age} years old.`);
  }
}

const person = new Person('Alice', 30);
person.greet();
```

### Inheritance

Inheritance allows a class to inherit properties and methods from another class.

```typescript
class Employee extends Person {
  position: string;

  constructor(name: string, age: number, position: string) {
    super(name, age);
    this.position = position;
  }

  describe() {
    console.log(`I am ${this.name}, ${this.age} years old, and I work as a ${this.position}.`);
  }
}

const employee = new Employee('Bob', 25, 'Developer');
employee.describe();
```

## Comparison Table

| Aspect          | Functional Programming | Object-Oriented Programming |
|-----------------|------------------------|-----------------------------|
| Focus           | Pure functions, immutability, higher-order functions | Classes, objects, inheritance |
| Data Structures | Arrays, maps, sets      | Objects, arrays, collections |
| Code Reusability| Functions, higher-order functions | Classes, inheritance |
| Complexity      | Often simpler and more concise | Can lead to complex class hierarchies |
| Performance     | Generally slower due to immutability | Can be more performant due to caching and optimization |

## When to Use Functional Programming

Functional programming is well-suited for the following scenarios:

- When you need to ensure that functions are pure and side-effect-free.
- When you want to write concise and readable code.
- When you are working with data streams or transformations.
- When you are developing concurrent or distributed systems.

## When to Use Object-Oriented Programming

Object-oriented programming is a good choice in the following situations:

- When you need to model complex systems with classes and objects.
- When you want to take advantage of inheritance and code reuse.
- When you are developing applications that require a high degree of modularity.
- When you are working with legacy systems that are heavily object-oriented.

## Actionable Takeaways

- Choose functional programming when you need pure functions, immutability, and higher-order functions.
- Choose object-oriented programming when you need to model complex systems with classes and objects.
- Be aware of the trade-offs between the two paradigms and choose the one that best suits your project's needs.
- Use TypeScript to leverage both paradigms and create robust, maintainable code.

By understanding the differences between functional programming and object-oriented programming, you can make informed decisions about how to structure your TypeScript projects. Whether you choose to use FP or OOP, or a combination of both, TypeScript provides the tools to help you achieve your goals.
