# TypeScript 4.0 and 5.0: New Features and Performance Improvements

## Introduction

TypeScript has become a crucial tool for modern web development, offering robust type checking and tooling that enhances JavaScript development. With each new version, TypeScript introduces new features and performance improvements that help developers write more efficient and maintainable code. In this article, we will compare TypeScript 4.0 and 5.0, highlighting the new features and performance enhancements that developers should be aware of.

## TypeScript 4.0: New Features

### 1. Non-Nullable by Default

TypeScript 4.0 introduced the `non-nullable` option, which enables non-nullable by default. This means that all variables are considered non-nullable unless explicitly marked as nullable.

```typescript
let x: number; // x is now non-nullable by default
```

### 2. Optional Chaining

Optional chaining (`?.`) is a new operator that allows you to safely access deeply nested properties of an object without having to check for `null` or `undefined` at each level.

```typescript
const user = {
  profile: {
    address: {
      street: '123 Main St',
    },
  },
};

console.log(user.profile?.address?.street); // '123 Main St'
```

### 3. Nullish Coalescing

The nullish coalescing operator (`??`) provides a way to provide a default value when a variable is `null` or `undefined`.

```typescript
const input = null;
const value = input ?? 'default value';
console.log(value); // 'default value'
```

## TypeScript 5.0: New Features

### 1. Intersection Types

Intersection types allow you to combine multiple types into a single type.

```typescript
interface Animal {
  name: string;
}

interface Mammal {
  hasFur: boolean;
}

type Dog = Animal & Mammal;
```

### 2. Nullish Coalescing Assignment

TypeScript 5.0 extends the nullish coalescing operator to work with variable assignments.

```typescript
let input = null;
input ??= 'default value';
console.log(input); // 'default value'
```

### 3. Nullish Coalescing in Conditional (Optional) Chaining

Optional chaining now supports the nullish coalescing operator within the chain.

```typescript
const user = {
  profile: {
    address: {
      street: null,
    },
  },
};

console.log(user.profile?.address?.street ?? 'not available');
```

## Performance Improvements

Both TypeScript 4.0 and 5.0 have introduced performance improvements that make the compiler faster and more efficient.

### 1. Faster Compiler

TypeScript 4.0 and 5.0 have been optimized to compile faster, which reduces the time developers wait for their code to be checked.

### 2. Improved Incremental Compilation

Incremental compilation allows TypeScript to only recompile the parts of the code that have changed, which can significantly reduce the compilation time for large projects.

## Comparison Table

| Feature/Improvement | TypeScript 4.0 | TypeScript 5.0 |
|---------------------|----------------|----------------|
| Non-Nullable by Default | Yes | N/A (already present) |
| Optional Chaining | Yes | N/A (already present) |
| Nullish Coalescing | Yes | Yes |
| Intersection Types | Yes | Yes |
| Nullish Coalescing Assignment | N/A (new in 5.0) | Yes |
| Nullish Coalescing in Conditional Chaining | N/A (new in 5.0) | Yes |
| Faster Compiler | Yes | Yes |
| Improved Incremental Compilation | Yes | Yes |

## Actionable Takeaways

- Start using non-nullable by default to prevent null errors.
- Utilize optional chaining and nullish coalescing to make your code more robust and readable.
- Take advantage of intersection types to combine multiple interfaces.
- Make use of the nullish coalescing assignment and conditional chaining for cleaner and more concise code.
- Take advantage of the faster compiler and improved incremental compilation to speed up your development process.

By staying up-to-date with the latest features and performance improvements in TypeScript, developers can write better code that is more efficient and maintainable.---

